geometric-fix-6e9bad15.symmetry_origin:
This interaction term arises from the principle of gauge invariance under the electromagnetic gauge group U(1). Specifically, when promoting the free field equations to include electromagnetic interaction, the ordinary derivative 
$$ \partial_\mu \quad \text{is replaced by the covariant derivative} \quad D_\mu = \partial_\mu + iqA_\mu \quad \text{(1/m)} 
$$ . The interaction Lagrangian 
$$ \mathcal{L}_{int} 
$$ is constructed to cancel the term generated by this replacement in the kinetic part of the Lagrangian, ensuring the full Lagrangian is gauge invariant.

geometric-fix-6e9bad15.limits_and_boundary:
As the electromagnetic field 
$$ A_\mu \quad \text{(V} \text{s/m)} \quad \text{approaches zero} 
$$ (i.e., in the absence of electromagnetic fields), the interaction Lagrangian 
$$ \mathcal{L}_{int} \quad \text{becomes zero} \quad (\text{J/m}^3) \quad \text{and the dynamics are governed solely by the free field Lagrangians.} \quad \text{Conversely, as the current density} \quad j^\mu \quad \text{(A/m}^2) \quad \text{or the field strength} \quad A_\mu \quad \text{approaches infinity} \quad \text{(physically unrealistic)} \quad \text{the interaction term becomes infinitely negative, suggesting unbound energy or instability within the system.}
